NNAAMMEE
       pgmkernel - generate a convolution kernel


SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
       ppggmmkkeerrnneell [--wweeiigghhtt==_n_u_m_b_e_r] [--mmaaxxvvaall==] {_s_i_z_e | _w_i_d_t_h _h_e_i_g_h_t}

       Minimum  unique abbreviation of option is acceptable.  You may use dou‐
       ble hyphens instead of single hyphen to denote options.   You  may  use
       white space in place of the equals sign to separate an option name from
       its value.



DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       This program is part of NNeettppbbmm(1).

       ppggmmkkeerrnneell generates a convolution kernel that you can use with  ppnnmmccoonn‐‐
       vvooll.   The kernel is one where the weight of each location is inversely
       proportional to its distance from the center of the kernel.

       ppggmmkkeerrnneell generates a PGM image of width and height _s_i_z_e if you specify
       one argument, or width _w_i_d_t_h and height _h_e_i_g_h_t if you specify two argu‐
       ments.

       ppggmmkkeerrnneell computes the convolution function K as follows.

              K(i,j) = 1 / ( 1 + w * sqrt(i^2 + j^2))

       where _w is a coefficient specified via the --wweeiigghhtt option.  _i and _j are
       measured  in  pixels.  K is zero everywhere beyond the specified kernel
       width and height.

       The sample values in the PGM output have this value scaled  and  biased
       using  the  protocol ppnnmmccoonnvvooll specifies for representing the real num‐
       bers K in PGM.



OOPPTTIIOONNSS
       In addition to the options common to all programs  based  on  libnetpbm
       (most notably --qquuiieett, see
        Common  Options ⟨index.html#commonoptions⟩ ), ppggmmkkeerrnneell recognizes the
       following command line options:



       --wweeiigghhtt==_n_u_m_b_e_r
              The distance from the center is weighted by this; a higher  num‐
              ber  means  the value falls off more quickly as you go away from
              the center.

              This must be a positive real number.

              The default is 6.0.


       --mmaaxxvvaall==_m_a_x_v_a_l
              The maxval for the PGM kernel.

              Default is 255.

              This option was new in Netpbm  10.65  (December  2013).   Before
              that, the maxval is always 255.





LLIIMMIITTAATTIIOONNSS
       The  computation  time is proportional to _w_i_d_t_h*_h_e_i_g_h_t.  This increases
       rapidly with the increase of the kernel size.  A better approach  could
       be using a FFT in these cases.


HHIISSTTOORRYY
       Before  Netpbm  10.65  (December  2013), the output was always in Plain
       (text) PGM format.  (Now, like standard Netpbm programs, the default is
       raw PGM and you can get Plain PGM with a --ppllaaiinn option).

       Before  Netpbm  10.65 (December 2013), this manual said negative values
       for --wweeiigghhtt were valid (as long as they were greater than  -1.0).   But
       the  program never worked with negative numbers and it isn't clear that
       the result would be useful, so --wweeiigghhtt is now required to  be  nonnega‐
       tive and the program fails gracefully if you specify a negative value.


